PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
.bd
wsnm
- print the contents of an object file


.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
wsnm [options] file.o ...


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Wsnm
prints what is inside an object file or an archive.  With no options it
prints the symbol table: for each symbol its value, the segment it
belongs to, whether it is global or local, and whether it is defined
there or referenced from elsewhere.

An archive is read member by member, each member's contents printed
under its name, so the same options that work on an object work on a
library.

It is the tool for the question "why will this not link", which is
usually answered by finding which object defines a symbol, or that
none does.

The options are:

.in +10
.ta 10
.ti -10
-v	Print the header: the size of each segment, the entry point,
and whether the file is relocatable.

.ti -10
-r	Print the relocation entries - each place the linker will
have to patch, and what it will patch it with.

.ti -10
-b	Print the text and data segments as a hexadecimal dump.

.ti -10
-d	Disassemble the text segment.

.ti -10
-g	Write assembler source that would produce the object again.

.in -10
.in -5
.bd
